# Whether every member of an interval with one-limb bounds is negative, and the interval is not
# empty (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface", the row of certainly_negative()): -inf < x_u < 0.
# Exact; false for the empty set. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_negative_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
output:        mask m
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       certain m lt x 0 x=xl,xu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the upper bound compared with 0 and -inf (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeIntervalPredicates.v interval_negative_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; certain states what comes out for every canonical operand
unused:        xl the upper bound alone tells: every member is at most it, and the interval is empty exactly when it is -inf

mi = neg(const inf)
a  = lt(xu, 0)
b  = lt(mi, xu)
m  = and(a, b)
